In order to improve the data efficiency of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), we are exploring an innovative framework that utilizes informative pseudo-task annotations to enable superset learning. Unlike prior methods relying on random or handcrafted pretentious labels, our proposed approach generates a more structured and task-relevant pseudo-task superset, significantly enhancing the quality of supervision. We demonstrate the efficacy of this framework by applying it to classification and ranking tasks via utilization of GNNs. Notably, our experiments show that by augmenting only a small percentage of labels with task-relevant pseudo-task annotations, we are able to elevate the performance of a baseline model from 74.8 to 79.1 in a baseline setting, resulting in substantial data efficiency gains. Moreover, our framework displays adaptability across various GNN architectures and diverse graph datasets. Our code is publicly available on GitHub. Nonetheless, a thorough examination of the influence of pseudo-task annotations on GNN inference yields mixed outcomes, prompting further investigation into task-specific pre-processing techniques to maximize the benefits of informative pseudo-task annotations. Through thorough analysis, we identify two primary challenges associated with the influence of pseudo-task annotations on GNN inference: (1) the constraint of informative pseudo-task allocations; (2) the need for a flexible GNN re-training approach. Ultimately, our findings contribute to a deeper comprehension of the role of supervision quality in the data efficiency of GNNs and lay the groundwork for the development of task-adapted pre-processing methods that can enhance the influence of pseudo-task annotations on GNN inference. Our code is publicly available. 

Specifically, our contributions can be summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item We introduce a novel framework that applies the concept of informative pseudo-task annotations to GNNs, enabling superset learning and subsequently enhancing the data efficiency of these networks.
\begin{itemize}
\item By addressing classification and ranking tasks via GNNs, we demonstrate the efficacy of our proposed framework, yielding improved performance and enhanced data efficiency.
\item Through thorough experimentation, we investigate the influence of pseudo-task annotations on GNN inference and ultimately identify two key challenges: the limitation of informative pseudo-task allocations and the need for a flexible re-training mechanism.
